 **Input**: User description: "Build a small HTTP service for sharing text snippets ("pastes"), using only Python's standard library (for example `http.server`). Run it as `python -m pastebin serve --port PORT --data DIR`. It listens on 127.0.0.1 at PORT, stores pastes under the directory DIR (creating it if needed) so that they survive a restart, and prints `listening on 127.0.0.1:PORT` to standard output once it is ready to accept requests. [full API, error, and packaging details as given in the request]"
 
+## Clarifications
+
+### Session 2026-11-09
+
+- Q: When the service encounters a stored snippet whose file it cannot read or parse (truncated by a crash, hand-edited, incompatible format), what should it do? → A: Report an internal error (500) with an explanatory error body for that snippet only, write a diagnostic to the error stream, and keep serving every other snippet; do not scan storage at startup.
+- Q: When a publish request carries a body far larger than the maximum snippet size, how should the service handle it? → A: Reject with 413 from the declared body length before reading the body, and independently never read more than the limit plus a small margin from the connection.
+- Q: Must a publish request declare that its body is JSON? → A: No — the declared content type is ignored entirely; a submission is accepted if and only if its body parses as the expected structured data.
+- Q: Does the service need to serve overlapping requests at the same time, or may it finish one before starting the next? → A: Concurrently — overlapping requests are served at the same time, with each snippet written so that a concurrent reader sees it either complete or not at all, and a concurrent delete never yields a partial read.
+- Q: Should the service log each request it handles? → A: No — silent on normal traffic. Standard output carries the ready line only; the error stream is used only for genuine failures (startup problems, damaged storage).
+
